-- title:
--   Partial Atkin–Lehner automorphism at p of L·ℚ(X₁(Mp))
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $M$ a nonzero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$; let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with an $L$-algebra structure making $L$ its fraction field, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$, with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K$. Let $j \in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157). Then there is an $L$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $K$ with the following six properties. (1) $\sigma j$ has Laurent expansion the image of [`ModularCurve.qExpand ℚ p ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25), the series obtained from $j(q)$ by multiplying all exponents by $p$. (2) For all $b \in K$, $b$ lies in [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L142), the subalgebra of elements of $K$ integral over the $A$-subalgebra generated by $j$, if and only if $\sigma b$ does. (3) For every valuation subring $W_0$ of $K$ consisting exactly of those $f$ admitting $x, y \in A[[q]]$ with $y$ of nonzero reduction modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ and $f \cdot y = x$ in $L((q))$, the pullback of $W_0$ along $\sigma$ is different from $W_0$, yet for every polynomial $P$ over $A$ with nonzero reduction both $P(j)$ and $P(j)^{-1}$ lie in that pullback. (4) For every $f$ in [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L305) (the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the divisor expansions at level $Mp$) whose coefficientwise image lies in $K$, $\sigma$ sends that image to the image of [`ModularCurve.atkinLehnerInvolutionFull M p f`](def/ModularCurve_AtkinLehnerPartial.html#L21), the chosen automorphism interchanging $j(q^d)$ and $j(q^{dp})$ for $d \mid M$ when such exists. (5) For $d, d'$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d' \equiv d \pmod M$ and $d'd \equiv 1 \pmod p$, and for any $L$-algebra automorphisms $\theta_d, \theta_{d'}$ of $K$ agreeing, under the identification of Laurent series, with the base changes to $L$ of the diamond automorphisms [`ModularCurve.diamondAut (M * p) d`](def/ModularCurve_X1Diamond.html#L66) and [`ModularCurve.diamondAut (M * p) d'`](def/ModularCurve_X1Diamond.html#L66), one has $\sigma \circ \theta_d \circ \sigma^{-1} = \theta_{d'}$ pointwise on $K$. (6) For $s$ a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism of $L$ and $b$ coprime to $p$ with $s\zeta = \zeta^b$, for $b'$ coprime to $Mp$ with $b' \equiv 1 \pmod M$ and $b' \equiv b \pmod p$, for any ring automorphism $S_s$ of $K$ acting on Laurent expansions by applying $s$ to coefficients, and any $\theta_{b'}$ as in (5) for $b'$, one has $S_s \circ \sigma \circ S_s^{-1} = \sigma \circ \theta_{b'}$ pointwise on $K$.
--
--   This provides the level-$p$ partial Atkin–Lehner automorphism $W_p$ on the function field $L\cdot\mathbb{Q}(X_1(Mp))$, together with the properties needed downstream: stability of the $j$-finite integral chart, the fact that it carries the Gauss branch above the generic point of the $j$-line modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to a different branch on which $j$ remains a unit, its identification with the Atkin–Lehner involution on the subfield coming from level-$Mp$ modular functions, and its conjugation rules against diamond automorphisms and against the coefficientwise action of $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$. It feeds the analysis of the two-chart integral model of $X_1(Mp)$ over $A$, namely the identification of its fibres and the construction of the Hecke operator $T_p$ on the special fibre.
-- source:
